<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rialto</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rialto is the central island group and district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, where the seat of government moved in the early 9th century after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a>'s attack in 810. It became Venice's commercial heart, with its market, banks and merchants' meeting places. The Rialto Bridge, completed in stone in 1591, was for centuries the only bridge across the Grand Canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rialto.jpg" alt="Rialto" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ponte_di_Rialto_Venice_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rialto lies in the sestiere of San Polo, on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. By the ninth century a small area in the middle of the Realtine Islands, on either side of a channel called the Rio Businiacus, was known as the Rivoaltus, meaning high bank. In time the Businiacus came to be called the Grand Canal, and the name Rialto came to refer only to the district on its left bank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The district gained in importance in 1097, when the city's market moved there. In the following century a bridge of boats was laid across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> to reach it, and this was soon replaced by the Rialto Bridge. The market grew into both a retail and a wholesale centre. Warehouses were built, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi">Fondaco dei Tedeschi</a> on the far side of the bridge, and shops selling luxury goods, banks on Rialto Square and insurance agencies opened. The city's tax offices and its abattoir were also in the Rialto.</p><p>A fire in 1514 destroyed most of the district's buildings, and the church of San Giacomo di Rialto was the only one to survive. The area was rebuilt gradually. The Fabriche Vechie date from this period and the Fabbriche Nuove from 1553, and the statue known as Il Gobbo di Rialto was carved in the same century.</p><p>The Rialto became a byword for trade in literature. In Shakespeare's The Merchant of Venice, Salanio opens Act III, Scene I by asking: &quot;What news on the Rialto?&quot; Elizabeth Barrett Browning wrote in Sonnet 19 of her Sonnets from the Portuguese that &quot;The soul's Rialto hath its merchandise&quot;. The poet and satirist Pietro Aretino, who lived opposite the market in the Palazzo Bollani, wrote in 1550 of his view across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>: &quot;So I can truly say that I enjoy the most beautiful street and the liveliest view in the world.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Rialto remains a busy retail quarter and the commercial heart of the city. The Erberia greengrocers' market is held there daily, and the fish market on the Campo della Pescheria sells shellfish, squid, cuttlefish, eels, crabs, octopus and lobsters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rialto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The seat of government then moved to the more defensible <b>Rialto</b> islands under Doge Agnello Participazio.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
